CAT = "oacpGrammar"; SEG = "GRM"
Q = [
("In the sentence \"Each of the officers ___ a radio\", which word fills the blank?", "carries", ["carry", "are carrying", "have carried"], "\"Each\" is singular, so it takes the singular verb \"carries.\" The plural noun \"officers\" is part of a phrase and does not change the subject."),
("In the sentence \"Neither the driver nor the passengers ___ wearing seatbelts\", which word fills the blank?", "were", ["was", "is", "has been"], "With \"neither... nor,\" the verb agrees with the nearer subject. \"Passengers\" is plural, so the verb is \"were,\" not \"was.\""),
("Which sentence keeps the verb tenses consistent?", "He opened the door and ran into the street.", ["He opened the door and runs into the street.", "He opens the door and ran into the street.", "He opened the door and is running to the street."], "A narrative report keeps its actions in the same tense. \"Opened\" and \"ran\" are both past; mixing in \"runs\" switches to the present."),
("In the sentence \"The squad ___ ready for its inspection\", which word fills the blank?", "was", ["were", "are", "have"], "\"Its\" shows that the squad is being treated as one unit, so the verb must be singular. \"Were\" would clash with the singular \"its.\""),
("In the sentence \"The sergeant asked Kim and ___ to stay late\", which word fills the blank?", "me", ["I", "myself", "mine"], "The pronoun is an object of \"asked,\" so the object form \"me\" is right. Drop \"Kim and\" to test it: \"asked I\" sounds wrong."),
("In the sentence \"The number of break-ins ___ risen this year\", which word fills the blank?", "has", ["have", "are", "were"], "\"The number\" is singular, so it takes \"has.\" The plural \"break-ins\" only names what is being counted."),
("In the sentence \"A number of witnesses ___ come forward\", which word fills the blank?", "have", ["has", "were", "does"], "\"A number of\" means \"several\" and takes a plural verb. \"Has\" fits \"the number of,\" which is a different phrase."),
("In the sentence \"The injured man was told to ___ still until help came\", which word fills the blank?", "lie", ["lay", "laid", "lain"], "\"Lie\" means to rest and takes no object. \"Lay\" means to put something down, so it needs an object."),
("In the sentence \"Kim and ___ stayed late to finish the report\", which word fills the blank?", "I", ["me", "myself", "us"], "The pronoun is part of the subject, so it takes the subject form \"I.\" Test it alone: \"me stayed late\" is clearly wrong."),
("Which sentence is free of a dangling modifier?", "Walking to the car, I noticed a broken window.", ["Walking to the car, a broken window was noticed.", "Walking to the car, the window was seen broken.", "Walking to the car, a broken window caught my eye."], "The opening phrase must describe the subject that follows it. Only \"I\" can be walking to the car; a window cannot walk."),
("In a formal report, which word completes the sentence correctly: The man ___ we arrested gave a false name?", "whom", ["who", "whose", "which"], "The pronoun is the object of \"arrested\" (we arrested him), so the object form \"whom\" is correct in formal writing. \"Who\" is the subject form."),
("In the sentence \"Yesterday the victim ___ on the couch for an hour\", which word fills the blank?", "lay", ["laid", "lied", "lain"], "The past tense of \"lie\" (to rest) is \"lay.\" \"Laid\" is the past of \"lay\" (to put down) and needs an object."),
("Which sentence is grammatically correct?", "Keep this between you and me for now.", ["Keep this between you and I for now.", "Keep this between yourself and I now.", "Keep this between you and myself now."], "\"Between\" is a preposition, so both pronouns after it take the object form: \"you and me.\" \"Between you and I\" is a common error."),
("In the sentence \"The two drivers handed ___ licences to the constable\", which word fills the blank?", "their", ["its", "his", "her"], "Two drivers is a plural antecedent, so it takes the plural pronoun their. His or her would refer to only one person, and its is used for things, not people."),
("In the sentence \"She ___ the evidence bag on the table\", which word fills the blank?", "laid", ["lay", "lain", "lied"], "\"Laid\" is the past tense of \"lay\" (to put down) and takes the object \"bag.\" \"Lay\" is the past of \"lie\" (to rest), and \"lied\" means told a falsehood."),
("In the sentence \"The suspect had ___ across the river before dawn\", which word fills the blank?", "swum", ["swam", "swimming", "swim"], "After \"had,\" use the past participle \"swum.\" \"Swam\" is the simple past and does not follow \"had.\""),
("Which sentence from the witness statement contains an error?", "I seen the truck leave the lot.", ["I saw the truck leave the lot early.", "I have seen that truck before.", "She had seen the truck twice."], "\"Seen\" is a past participle and needs a helper such as \"have\" or \"had.\" On its own the simple past is \"saw.\""),
("In the sentence \"By midnight he had ___ four beers\", which word fills the blank?", "drunk", ["drank", "drinking", "drunken"], "After \"had,\" use the past participle \"drunk.\" \"Drank\" is the simple past, and \"drunken\" is an adjective."),
("Which sentence correctly says the officer wrote about twenty tickets?", "The officer wrote nearly twenty tickets.", ["The officer nearly wrote twenty tickets.", "Nearly the officer wrote twenty tickets.", "The officer wrote twenty nearly tickets."], "\"Nearly\" should sit next to the number it limits. \"Nearly wrote\" means the officer almost wrote tickets but did not."),
("Which option is a complete sentence?", "The suspect fled because he saw the cruiser.", ["Because he saw the cruiser parked outside.", "The suspect, who saw the cruiser parked outside.", "Seeing the cruiser parked outside the store."], "A complete sentence needs a subject and a main verb. \"Because he saw the cruiser parked outside\" is a dependent clause and cannot stand alone."),
("Which option fixes the run-on \"The light was red the driver did not stop\"?", "The light was red, but the driver did not stop.", ["The light was red, the driver did not stop.", "The light was red but, the driver did not stop.", "The light was red the driver, did not stop."], "A comma plus a joining word such as \"but\" correctly links two complete sentences. A comma alone creates a comma splice."),
("Which sentence uses parallel structure?", "He was told to stop, turn around and kneel.", ["He was told to stop, turning around and kneel.", "He was told stopping, to turn around and kneel.", "He was told to stop, turn around and kneeling."], "Items in a series should share the same form, here three base verbs after \"to.\" Mixing in \"turning\" or \"kneeling\" breaks the pattern."),
("In the sentence \"There were ___ calls this week than last week\", which word fills the blank?", "fewer", ["less", "lesser", "smaller"], "Use \"fewer\" with things you can count, such as calls. \"Less\" is for amounts you cannot count, such as time."),
("In the sentence \"The repair took ___ time than we expected\", which word fills the blank?", "less", ["fewer", "lesser", "few"], "Time is an amount, not separate countable items, so \"less\" is correct. \"Fewer\" is for countable nouns."),
("In the sentence \"The new recruit performed ___ on the fitness test\", which word fills the blank?", "well", ["good", "healthy", "greatly"], "\"Performed\" is an action verb, so it takes the adverb \"well.\" \"Good\" is an adjective and describes nouns."),
("In the sentence \"Of the three witnesses, Ana gave the ___ account\", which word fills the blank?", "clearest", ["clearer", "clearly", "more clear"], "With three or more, use the superlative \"clearest.\" \"Clearer\" compares only two, and \"clearly\" is an adverb, which cannot describe the noun \"account.\""),
("In the sentence \"The woman ___ called 911 stayed on the line\", which word fills the blank?", "who", ["whom", "which", "whose"], "The pronoun is the subject of \"called\" (she called), so the subject form \"who\" is right. \"Whom\" is used for objects."),
("Which comparison contains a grammatical error?", "This report is more better than mine.", ["This report is far better than the last.", "This report is clearer than mine.", "This report is the best one so far."], "\"Better\" is already a comparative, so adding \"more\" doubles it. \"More better\" should simply be \"better.\""),
]
